## 6. Worked Example: Epistemic Path Governance

### 6.1 The originating observation

The operator then identified a second problem:

> Deeper inference is expensive. The more branches a model explores inside one problem, the more compute it consumes. There should be an efficient, predictable, rules-based way to decide which branches deserve further exploration.

Again, the operator did not provide a complete framework.

The existing shared ontology made it possible to infer the likely architecture.

# Epistemic Path Governance (EPG)

### 6.2 Core idea

EBG asks:

> Can this inferential bridge responsibly carry the weight placed on it?

EPG asks:

> Is this path important enough to justify additional reasoning effort?

### 6.3 Governing principle

> **Do not spend equal compute on branches with unequal consequence.**

A model should prioritize branches according to:

* materiality;
* probability of changing the synthesis;
* accessibility of additional evidence;
* weakness of the load-bearing edge;
* compute cost;
* and opportunity cost.

A useful heuristic is:

[
\text{Branch Priority}
\propto
\frac{
\text{Materiality}
\times
\text{Probability of Updating the Synthesis}
\times
\text{Evidence Accessibility}
}{
\text{Compute Cost}
}
]

### 6.4 Proposed operating sequence

1. Identify the decision target.
2. Map the minimum sufficient tree.
3. Find the strongest supporting path.
4. Find the strongest restrictive path.
5. Identify the weakest load-bearing edge.
6. Expand that edge first.
7. Prune branches that cannot change the conclusion.
8. Stop when additional inference no longer changes action.

### 6.5 Why this matters

Intelligence is not merely the ability to infer deeply.

It is the ability to decide **where depth is worth paying for**.

That is a compute-stewardship problem.

It is also an Intelligent Yielding problem.

