## 8. Worked Example: Cold Notebook, Hot Triggers

### 8.1 The originating observation

The operator noted that formal benchmarking resources are limited.

His current discovery instrument is meta-epistemic sensitivity:

> the mechanic’s tactile feel.

That sensitivity detects recurring failure signatures such as:

* caveats spreading beyond the weak joint;
* premature confidence release;
* confidence inhibition after a claim has survived sufficient pressure;
* register flattening;
* reopening settled questions;
* context drift;
* or failure to archive exhausted tangents.

The thread then produced a maintenance-layer concept.

# Cold Notebook, Hot Triggers

### 8.2 Core idea

A formal failure-mode notebook may be useful.

But it should not remain continuously hot inside the main reasoning context.

The efficient design is:

[
\text{External failure log}
\rightarrow
\text{Compressed active watchlist}
\rightarrow
\text{Triggered retrieval when needed}
]

### 8.3 Three layers

| Layer                     | Function                                                     |               Attentional cost |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -----------------------------: |
| **Active watchlist**      | Current risks worth monitoring continuously                  |                       Very low |
| **Failure-mode registry** | Structured record of recurring patterns and mitigations      |                Cold by default |
| **Incident archive**      | Examples, screenshots, transcripts, and detailed postmortems | Retrieved only when diagnosing |

### 8.4 Why this matters

The notebook should help prevent ELT from regressing into its early rule-stack phase.

The process should not be:

> Observe failure → add another rule.

It should be:

> Observe failure → classify pattern → test recurrence → locate root cause → determine whether an existing mechanism can absorb the fix → promote only if load-bearing.

